<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Layout of the OntoBoard tab, in Protege's mdock format.

  VSNode means a VERTICAL SPLITTER, so its children sit side by side in columns; HSNode is a
  horizontal splitter and stacks its children in rows. The names describe the divider, not the
  arrangement, which is the opposite of what most people expect - this file used HSNode and the
  hierarchy appeared underneath the canvas. VerticalSplitter sets a W_RESIZE cursor and
  HorizontalSplitter an N_RESIZE one, which settles it; ViewConfigTest pins the orientation.

  A CNode holding several Components renders them as tabs, one visible at a time. All six entity
  views therefore share the left column instead of competing for space, the same arrangement
  Protege's own Entities tab uses (viewconfig-entitiestab.xml).

  THE AXIOMS PANE UNDER THE TREES. Until 1.122.0 this tab had no view that showed anything about
  the selected term: six trees and a canvas, and nowhere to read what a class actually says. A
  reader clicking a term on the board got its name selected in a tree and no axioms anywhere.

  org.protege.editor.owl.ManchesterSyntaxFrameView is one id that prints the whole axiom frame of
  whatever is selected - class, object property, data property, individual, datatype, annotation
  property - because it is an AbstractOWLSelectionViewComponent over the same global
  OWLSelectionModel that SchemaCanvasView.pushSelectionToProtege writes to and the trees opposite
  read. It needs no per-type variants and no CardLayout: one scrolling text area that follows the
  selection. Declared identically in Protege 5.5.0 and 5.6.9 (plugin.xml:730 and :740), so it
  resolves on both hosts.

  WHY NOT A THIRD COLUMN, which is the obvious arrangement and is what Protege's own per-type tabs
  use. The canvas column cannot afford it. The toolbar was measured at 777px minimum in 1.116.0
  after three rounds of shortening (see SchemaCanvasView's own comment: it wanted 1261px and began
  clipping at 1030, and a 1440px window gives this column 857px). Taking the canvas from 0.72 to
  0.52 for a details column gives the toolbar 619px in that same window - 158px short - and the
  clipping it silently returns to is a button that is laid out past the right edge, unpainted and
  unclickable. Stacked here instead, the canvas keeps every pixel of its width.

  WHY NOT org.protege.editor.owl.SelectedEntityView, the card view Protege's Entities tab uses.
  It spends its own height on an inner HSNode (0.375/0.625 for annotations over description), so
  in a pane this narrow it is two cramped halves rather than one readable one; and its six nested
  ViewsPanes use hard-coded memento ids, so with Protege's Entities tab also open the two
  instances share saved inner layouts and dragging a divider in one moves the other. The two views
  are complements - that one edits frames, this one reads axioms - and reading is what was missing.
-->
<layout>
    <VSNode splits="0.28 0.72">
        <HSNode splits="0.46 0.54">
        <CNode>
            <Component label="Classes">
                <Property id="pluginId" value="org.protege.editor.owl.OWLAssertedClassHierarchy"/>
            </Component>
            <Component label="Object properties">
                <Property id="pluginId" value="org.protege.editor.owl.OWLObjectPropertyTree"/>
            </Component>
            <Component label="Data properties">
                <Property id="pluginId" value="org.protege.editor.owl.OWLDataPropertyTree"/>
            </Component>
            <Component label="Annotation properties">
                <Property id="pluginId" value="org.protege.editor.owl.OWLAnnotationPropertyTree"/>
            </Component>
            <Component label="Datatypes">
                <Property id="pluginId" value="org.protege.editor.owl.OWLDatatypeList"/>
            </Component>
            <Component label="Individuals">
                <Property id="pluginId" value="org.protege.editor.owl.OWLIndividualsList"/>
            </Component>
        </CNode>
        <!-- What the selected term says, in a CNode of its own so it is beside the trees rather
             than one more tab among them: a reader who clicks a class in the tree wants to see
             the tree AND what the class says. Two Components, so mdock renders them as tabs.

             SelectedEntityView FIRST, because it is the editable one. It is the same view
             Protege's own Entities tab uses - annotations, the class or property description,
             usage and characteristics, each an editable frame with its own add button, and it
             switches by entity type on its own because it is a CardLayout over all six. Shipping
             only the Manchester view made this pane a reader: it prints the whole axiom frame of
             anything selected and lets you change none of it.

             Both are kept because they answer different questions. The frames are for editing one
             assertion; the Manchester text is the whole entity in one scrollable block, which is
             what you want when you are reading rather than changing. -->
        <CNode>
            <Component label="Annotations and description">
                <Property id="pluginId" value="org.protege.editor.owl.SelectedEntityView"/>
            </Component>
            <Component label="Axioms">
                <Property id="pluginId" value="org.protege.editor.owl.ManchesterSyntaxFrameView"/>
            </Component>
        </CNode>
        </HSNode>
        <!-- The canvas, with the whole of the right-hand column.

             For five releases the sheet editor was stacked underneath it here, at 45% of this
             column. That was not a design: putting both in ONE CNode, as tabs, so each could
             use the full column - the arrangement the six entity views opposite use - is a
             thing Protege does not do for plugin views. With both in one CNode it instantiated
             the canvas and silently dropped the sheet editor, which was then registered,
             resolvable, and never constructed; the self-test caught it only because it had
             just been taught to require every view plugin.xml registers. So they were stacked,
             and both were built, and both were cramped.

             The sheet editor now has a workspace tab of its own - viewconfig-sheetstab.xml -
             which is the mechanism that gives each of them the window. ViewConfigTest pins
             that this column holds the canvas alone and that the sheet editor is in that other
             config, because "tidying" the two back together is the obvious thing to try. -->
        <CNode>
            <Component label="Schema Canvas">
                <Property id="pluginId" value="${project.artifactId}.SchemaCanvasView"/>
            </Component>
        </CNode>
    </VSNode>
</layout>
